WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:12.520
Yo! What it do? This week on Thug
Notes we gettin lovestruck with

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:14.940
"Romeo and Juliet" by William Shakespeare.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:25.439
Up in the swanky part of Verona, two families called the Montagues and the Capulets been beefin since foreva.

00:00:25.439 --> 00:00:30.510
After dodging an all-out war in the streets,
Big Daddy Montague and his boo Lady Montague

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:36.100
start talkin bout their emo son Romeo, who got it bad for some fox Rosaline,

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:43.820
but Rosaline ain’t feelin it, and now Romeo mopin around like he dun lost his nuts. So Romeo’s homieos Benvolio and

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:49.600
Mercutio try to get his mind off that trick
by crashing a party over at the Capulet's crib.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:54.639
But as soon as Romeo peeps the most-
tappable ass of Juliet Capulet, he forgets

00:00:54.639 --> 00:00:59.259
all about dat Rosaline mess and steps
up to holla, like a champ.

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:06.800
Romeo loves himself some fast women, so he drops his A-game, gets a lil lip action, and snags those digits.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:10.680
Get it, Romeo.  
Then Romeo creeps up to Juliet’s balcony.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:14.300
Turns out, she batsh*t crazy in love
with him too!

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:21.020
But since their families got so much beef, they gotta keep it all on the DL. Next day, Romeo and Juliet run off

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:24.660
to local preacher man Friar Lawrence’s
spot and get hitched.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:29.120
Later, Benvolio and Mercutio takin
it easy, when Tybalt,

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:34.300
the baddest mofo of all da Capulets, steps up and starts talkin sh*t.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:40.560
Mercutio boot up and start scrappin with Tybalt, til Romeo busts in and tries to break it up.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:45.760
But Romeo just gets in the way, and Tybalt shanks Mercutio. Damn.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:51.840
Romeo goes from whiny b*tch to the hardest thug in the streets and straight murks Tybalt.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:55.070
The Prince of Verona had it with all this gang violence.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:59.920
So he boots Romeo right outta town.
When Juliet’s mama say she gotta marry some

00:01:59.929 --> 00:02:04.399
candy-ass scrub named
Paris, Juliet gets all torn up and hits up

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:07.900
Friar Lawrence for some help.
Fry-Daddy comes with a plan to fake

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:12.380
Juliet’s death by giving her some juice
that makes her take a long-ass nap.

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:18.880
Then he gonna write Romeo a letter, sayin all he has to do is play it cool, and once Juliet wakes up,

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:25.120
Romeo will have a lifetime supply of poontang.
Problem is, the letter never makes it to Romeo.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:31.400
So when he hears Juliet bit the
dust, he thinks this sh*t's fo real. Romeo straight flips.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:37.080
So he rolls up to Juliet’s crypt, peeps her layin stiff, and throws back some poison.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:43.660
When Juliet wakes up and sees his dead body, she gets all crunk, grabs a knife, and kills herself.

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:46.060
Teenagers, man. Damn.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:54.420
Peep this motif, blood. Willy
Shakes flippin opposites all up in this play:

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:58.640
love/hate, youth/age, life/death — I ain’t
even trippin.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:04.640
Even characters got opposites. For
example, while Benvolio be all chill and peace-lovin,

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:11.740
Tybalt always comin out hard, tryin to throw down. Sh*t you can even peep these paradoxes in tha text, B.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:24.960
But Willy ain't just playin — all these clashing opposites makin dat dramatic action mo intense. Listen up, patna.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:29.720
The full title of this play is “The Most Excellent and Lamentable Tragedy of Romeo and Juliet.”

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:33.680
'Cept, is it really a tragedy? Some homies don’t think so.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:37.340
See, in most tragedies, the characters got somethin in their dome that’s

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:41.140
so f**ked up, that no matter what, they gonna end up feelin the hurt.

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:43.719
Peeps call this, “The
Tragic Flaw.”

00:03:43.719 --> 00:03:48.280
Thang is, some thugs say Romeo and
Juliet ain’t rollin with no tragic flaws.

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:51.100
It ain’t their fault that sh*t gets so cray.

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:56.000
But what is tragic is that something else is trippin up their game — chance.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.560
All throughout this play, fate doin our lovers straight dirty.

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:06.260
Just look at all these ridiculous coincidences: dat letter don’t find Romeo, the Friar too late in stoppin him,

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:11.220
Juliet wakes up right after Romeo bit the big one.
I could go on, playa.

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:14.310
So when the prologue sayin that these kids be “star-crossed,”

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:17.290
maybe it’s cuz the heavens got it in fo these fools.

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:21.760
But maybe it ain’t fate so much as it is kids actin like stupid lil sh*ts.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:24.700
Some
say Romeo and Juliet’s tragic flaw be a

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:29.760
love all insane in the membrane. And they
willin to do anything to keep it real.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.180
Even Friar Lawrence say that sh*t a sin.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:55.300
Could be they ain't sinnin at all, but that the world that they live in just too twisted to

00:04:55.300 --> 00:05:00.380
deal with a love as pure as theirs. Maybe it ain’t our lovers that got the flaws, son.

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:06.140
Maybe it’s the world. But all scholars agree it would be
a damn tragedy if you didn’t subscribe.

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:08.140
Peace, yall!

